+The globe and its clouds are seen through the same air (aerial perspective, Hillaire 2020 §6). Their
+shaders (src/render/scene.ts, with `AERIAL` from atmosphere.ts) march the view ray from the camera
+to the Earth's sphere — to the cloud layer's sphere, 9 km up, for the clouds — with the same model
+and tables, single scattering with the Earth's shadow plus the multiple-scattering term, and show
+the surface's light dimmed by the transmittance T of that stretch with the light L it scatters in
+added: col·T + L·E, E the sky pass's illuminance. The stretch is solved from the camera through the
+ray's perigee, as the sky's is, and ends on the sphere, not on the meshes' facets (up to ~3 km under
+it), which would scallop the haze at the limb. The globe takes 10 samples and the clouds 6, crowded
+quadratically towards the far end, where the air is densest on any ray that ends on the ground; a
+cloud texel the map leaves clear is dropped before the march. Against an independent fine integral
+of the same single-scattering model (tests/render-aerial.test.ts) the error in the scattered light
+is at most 4.7 % of a ray's brightest channel from 400 km (RMS 1.4 %; the worst rays graze the limb
+with the sun low ahead of them) and 1.6 % from 30 km (RMS 0.6 %), in the transmittance at most
+4.5 % and 1.9 %; for the clouds' six samples 4.6 % and 3.5 %. From orbit this is the blue haze over
+the disc, thickening towards the limb into the sky's own limb glow; from the stratosphere, the
+distant ground fading into the horizon sky, which until then it met at about three times the
+globe's brightness; none on the night side, and the terminator's twilight from the model. The globe's
+Fresnel rim, the gradient sky's stand-in for this, is off under the scattering sky. The sky pass no
+longer marches a ray that ends more than 10 km inside the Earth's sphere: the opaque globe covers it
+and carries that haze itself.
+
+The sun's illuminance in the sky's units is 8 on the ground, set so that noon reads like the
+gradient sky it replaced, and falls with the camera's height (the ground factor of src/render/sky.ts,
+from 12 to 72 km) to 3.3 in space, the scene's own sun that lights the globe, the clouds and the
+vehicles; the sky pass and the aerial perspective take the same value, so the haze meets the sky at
+the horizon and at the limb. At 8 from orbit the haze, 2.4 times what lights the ground under it,
+veiled the whole disc in pale blue.
+
